### Step 2: Choose a starter prompt

👉 Click on the prompt title to reveal the prompt. Copy and paste it into the LLM of your choice.

*Prompts have [ ] for you to fill in with specific instructions and context from your classroom.* We recommend that you choose a realistic scenario from your classroom.

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Planning and Preparation</strong>: Differentiating an assessment</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an experienced teacher and instructional designer.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I am creating a formative assessment on <strong>[topic/standard]</strong>.
Here is my original assessment:
<strong>[Paste or describe the assessment]</strong>
My goal is to differentiate this assessment so all students can access the same learning goal:
<strong>[learning objective/standard]</strong>
Please create:
- A scaffolded version (for students who need support)
- A grade-level version
- An extension version (for advanced learners)
For each version:
- Keep it aligned to the same learning objective
- Adjust language, structure, and supports as needed
- Include clear student directions
- Maintain meaningful thinking (not just simplification)
Also include:
- A short explanation of how each version supports different learners
- Suggested supports (sentence starters, vocabulary, graphic organizers, etc.)
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Communication and Materials Creation</strong>: Writing a parent email</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an experienced teacher helping draft communication with families.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I need to write an email to a parent/guardian about:
<strong>[purpose of the email]</strong>
Here are the key points to include:
- <strong>[Key point 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 2]</strong>
- <strong>[Key point 3]</strong>
Additional context (tone, relationship, sensitivity, etc.):
<strong>[optional context]</strong>
Please write an email that:
- Is clear, concise, and easy to understand
- Uses a respectful, supportive, and professional tone
- Reflects collaboration with families
Then provide:
- 1–2 alternative ways to phrase key parts of the message
- Suggestions to improve clarity or tone if needed
</div>
</details>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; margin-right: 0%; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em;"><strong>Reflection and Improvement
</strong>: Getting feedback on a lesson plan
</summary>
<div style="font-size:1em; line-height:1.5; white-space:pre-wrap;">
You are an instructional coach providing actionable feedback.
I teach <strong>[grade level]</strong> <strong>[subject]</strong>, and I want feedback on a lesson about:
<strong>[lesson topic]</strong>
Here is my lesson plan:
<strong>[Paste or describe the lesson]</strong>
My goals for the lesson:
- <strong>[Goal 1]</strong>
- <strong>[Goal 2]</strong>
After teaching it, I noticed:
<strong>[what worked / what didn’t / student challenges]</strong>
Please give feedback on:
- Alignment to the learning goals
- Clarity of instructions
- Level of rigor and student thinking
- Opportunities for differentiation
Then suggest:
- Specific improvements I could make
- 1–2 alternative strategies to address student challenges
Keep feedback practical and focused on changes I can realistically implement.
</div>
</details>

### Step 3: Explore and Refine

As you explore, use these strategies to improve and refine your prompting:

